    "done_when": "A fenced codex run can persist a refreshed login, so the first token refresh no longer burns the shared login. Measured on 2026-09-27: every fenced codex run gets the operator's single /home/ITER/mcintos/.codex/auth.json bound read-only at CODEX_HOME/auth.json (reckon/_backends.py, _harness_credential_binds). That file was last written 2026-09-17. codex-luna run r-20260926T204353115391 then failed with \"Your access token could not be refreshed because your refresh token was already used\" and a 401. The explanation: OAuth refresh tokens are single-use, and a fenced refresh rotated the token server-side but could not write it back. The lead chose to bind ONLY that one file read-write; the rest of ~/.codex stays sealed.\n\nStep 1 is measure first. Establish how the installed codex binary (/home/ITER/mcintos/.npm/packages/bin/codex) writes auth.json on refresh: in place (open with O_TRUNC or O_WRONLY on the same inode) or by writing a temp file and renaming it over auth.json. Rename onto a file bind mount fails with EBUSY or EXDEV inside bubblewrap. Use strace -f -e trace=openat,rename,renameat,renameat2 on a codex command that rewrites auth.json, for example a login status or refresh path, run against a COPY of auth.json in a scratch CODEX_HOME under tmp_path. Never run it against the real ~/.codex, and never trigger a real refresh of the operator's token. Record the syscalls in logs/codex-auth-write-mode.log. If no command rewrites auth.json without a real refresh, say so and use the codex source or docs to establish the write mode, citing it.\n\nStep 2: if the write is in place, change the fence so CODEX_HOME/auth.json is a read-write bind of /home/ITER/mcintos/.codex/auth.json, and the protected read-only overlay of ~/.codex stays in force for every other path. If the write is a rename, do not change the bind. Write status: blocked with the measurement, because the lead must then choose another design.\n\nTests, in tests/test_fenced_codex_can_persist_its_login.py, in tmp_path (the pytest fixture) with a synthesised operator home:\n- The composed fenced argv for a codex backend carries a read-write bind (--bind, not --ro-bind) for the operator auth.json at CODEX_HOME/auth.json.\n- It still carries the read-only protection for the rest of the operator's ~/.codex.\n- No copy of auth.json is ever written into the run directory.\nNegative control: restore --ro-bind; the first assertion must fail.\ntests/test_each_run_owns_its_harness_home.py, tests/test_fence_never_binds_the_main_checkout.py and tests/test_fence_is_on_again.py add no failures against the base, with pytest basetemp outside any git repository. Logs go under the run directory, each headed with revision, tree and the literal command. The change is committed in this worktree.",
    "estimated_hours": null,
    "goal": "Let a fenced codex run write back a refreshed login through a read-write bind of the one operator auth.json, after measuring that codex rewrites it in place.",
